   "text": "This is obeticholic acid, which is 6\u03b1\u2011ethyl\u2011chenodeoxycholic acid. The drawing shows a steroid core with a 3\u2011OH, a 7\u2011OH, a 6\u2011ethyl group, two angular methyls and a pentanoic acid side chain.\n\nReading the stereochemistry:\n- The drawing is rotated and flipped relative to the usual steroid orientation, which swaps wedges and hashes.\n- The two methyls are hashed in this view, which corresponds to \u03b2.\n- The 3\u2011OH, 7\u2011OH and 6\u2011ethyl are wedged, which corresponds to \u03b1.\n- The H at C5 is hashed, which corresponds to 5\u03b2, as in bile acids.\n- The 8\u2011 and 9\u2011H and the other ring\u2011fusion H atoms are shown in the usual steroid configuration.\n\nI did not derive every stereocentre independently from the picture. I built the SMILES from the chenodeoxycholic acid skeleton and added the 6\u03b1\u2011ethyl group. The configuration at C6 comes from my recollection of the obeticholic acid SMILES rather than from a separate check against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H](CCC(=O)O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H]2[C@@]1(CC[C@H]3[C@H]2[C@@H]([C@H](CC)[C@H]4[C@@]3(CC[C@H](C4)O)C)O)C"
